--TEST--
A shape field write lands on the field it names and is checked against the concrete field's own declared type — `rule:types/shape-type` and `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/erased-member-access`'s write half, mirroring the read: a shape has no declaring
// class, so the write is keyed on the *name* rather than on a fixed offset.
class Move {
    // `{y: int}` lays `y` at slot 0; the `{x: int, y: int}` value lays it at
    // slot 1, because a shape's slots are its field names sorted.
    public static function bumpY({y: int} $p): void {
        $p->y = 9;
    }

    // The same widening over a refcounted field: `label` is slot 0 in the
    // view and slot 1 in the value. What the slot held is released by the
    // write, so nothing leaks and nothing is freed twice.
    public static function relabel({label: string} $t): void {
        $t->label = "right";
    }

    // § 3 compares a shape's field types by ordinary assignability, so
    // `{n: int}` satisfies `{n: int|string}` — and a shape value is aliased
    // rather than copied. A write through the wider view is therefore checked
    // against what the concrete value declares, and throws rather than
    // leaving a `string` in a slot the narrow view loads as an `int`.
    public static function widen({n: int|string} $p): string {
        try {
            $p->n = "s";
            return "not refused";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

// Through the value's own shape: the slot the checker resolved is the one the
// value uses, and the runtime's hint hits on the first try.
var $point = {x: 1, y: 2};
$point->x = 5;
echo $point->x, " ", $point->y, "\n";

// Through the narrower view: the write reaches `y`, and `x` is untouched —
// which is exactly what a slot-indexed write would have got wrong.
Move::bumpY($point);
echo $point->x, " ", $point->y, "\n";

var $tagged = {alpha: "a", label: "left"};
Move::relabel($tagged);
echo $tagged->alpha, " ", $tagged->label, "\n";

var $whole = {n: 1};
echo Move::widen($whole), "\n";
--EXPECT--
5 2
5 9
a right
`$shape{n}` declares field `n` as int, so a string cannot be written to it
